WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.040
Today is going to be all about simulating&nbsp;
virtual bunnies. What kinds of bunnies?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.400
Bunnies in an hourglass with granular&nbsp;
material, bunnies in a mixing drum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.480
bunnies that disappear, we’ll&nbsp;
try to fix this one too.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:28.000
So, what was all this footage? Well, this is&nbsp;
a followup work to the amazing Monolith paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.880
What is Monolith? It is a technique that helps&nbsp;
fixing commonly occurring two-way coupling issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.800
in physics simulations. Okay, that sounds great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:46.240
but what does two-way coupling mean? It means that&nbsp;
here, the boxes are allowed to move the smoke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:53.120
and the added two-way coupling part means that&nbsp;
now, the smoke is also allowed to blow away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.320
the boxes. This previous work makes&nbsp;
can simulate these phenomena properly.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:04.320
It also makes sure that when thrown at the&nbsp;
wall, things stick correctly, and a ton of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:12.880
other goodies too. So, this previous method shows&nbsp;
a lot of strength. Now, I hear you asking, Károly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:19.920
can this get even better? And the answer is yes,&nbsp;
yes it can! That’s exactly why we are here today.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.640
This new paper improves this&nbsp;
technique to work better in cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:31.680
where we have a lot of friction. For instance,&nbsp;
it can simulate how some of these tiny bunnies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:38.560
get squeezed through the hourglass, and get&nbsp;
showered by this sand-like granular material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:45.840
It can also simulate how some of them remain stuck&nbsp;
up there because of the frictional contact. Good.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.800
Now, have a look at this, with this one, with&nbsp;
an earlier technique, we start with one bunny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:58.320
and we end up with….wait a minute. That&nbsp;
volume is not one bunny amount of volume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.800
This is what we call the volume dissipation&nbsp;
problem. I wonder if we can get our bunny back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:12.480
with the new technique. What do you think? Well,&nbsp;
let’s see…one bunny goes in, friction happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:22.000
and…yes! One bunny amount of volume comes out of&nbsp;
the simulation. Then, we a bunch of them into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.480
mixing drum in the next experiment where their&nbsp;
tormenting shall continue. This is also a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:35.600
challenging scene because we have over 70 thousand&nbsp;
particles, rubbing against each other. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:43.280
just look at that. The new technique is so robust&nbsp;
that there are no issues whatsoever. Loving it!

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:50.400
So, what is all this simulation math good for?&nbsp;
Well, for instance, it helps us set up a scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.960
where we get a great deal of artistic freedom. For&nbsp;
instance, we can put this glass container with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:04.800
water between two walls, and, look carefully! Yes,&nbsp;
we apply a little leftward force to this wall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.880
And, since this technique can&nbsp;
simulate what is going to happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.880
we can create an imaginary world where only&nbsp;
our creativity is the limit. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.400
we can make a world in which there is just a&nbsp;
tiny bit of friction to slow down the fall.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.120
Or, we can create a world&nbsp;
with a ton more friction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.440
and now, we have so much friction&nbsp;
going on that the weight of the liquid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.200
cannot overcome anymore, and thus, the cube&nbsp;
is quickly brought to rest between the walls.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:41.920
Luckily, since our bunny still exists, we can&nbsp;
proceed onto the next experiment, where we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:48.560
drop it onto a pile of granular material. This&nbsp;
previous method did not do too well in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:54.720
as the bunny sinks down. And if you think&nbsp;
that cannot possibly get any worse, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:01.520
I have some news for you. It can. How? Look!&nbsp;
With a different previous technique, it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:09.840
even get to sink in, because it crashes when it&nbsp;
would need to perform these calculations. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.840
let’s see if the new method&nbsp;
can save the day, and…yes!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.920
Great! It indeed can help our little&nbsp;
bunny remain on top of things.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:26.880
So, let’s pop the Scholarly question -&nbsp;
how much do we wait for such a simulation?

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.360
The hourglass experiment takes&nbsp;
about 5 minutes per frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:38.160
while the rotating drum experiment takes about&nbsp;
half of that, 2.5 minutes. So, it takes a while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.640
Why? Of course, because many of these scenes&nbsp;
contain tens of thousands of particles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:50.800
and almost all of them are in constant frictional&nbsp;
interaction with almost all the others at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.640
same time, and the algorithm mustn’t miss&nbsp;
any of these interactions. All of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.080
have to be simulated. And the fact that through&nbsp;
the power of computer graphics research we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:09.600
simulate all of these in reasonable amount of time&nbsp;
is an absolute miracle. What a time to be alive!

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:16.480
And as always, you know what’s coming. Yes, please&nbsp;
do not forget to invoke the First Law of Papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.040
which says says that research is a&nbsp;
process. Do not look at where we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.080
look at where we will be two more papers&nbsp;
down the line. Granular materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:32.400
and frictional contact in a matter of seconds&nbsp;
perhaps? Well, sign me up for this one!

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.960
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

